  "result": "The gate returned **OK** for `NRS_484B_403_2` on the second of six allowed runs: 9 witnesses, 13 names, no compositions.\n\n**The rule**\n- **Subject:** a member of the vehicle aggregate `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`.\n- **When it applies:** at some moment in the 10 s window behind the judged moment, the vehicle is turning (yaw rate above the tolerance) under a `no_u_turn` sign whose conditions hold. \"Under the sign\" means one of two things:\n  - the sign governs a region the vehicle is in; or\n  - the vehicle is in an intersection it entered from a lane the sign governs, and that lane leads into that intersection.\n- **What it requires:** no U-turn is recognized at that moment.\n\n**What changed between runs:** the first run rejected every witness. The checker requires each heading to match the previous heading turned by the yaw rate. My 30°/s turns produced headings that can't be written exactly, so I rebuilt every turn at 90°/s, which keeps headings on north, south, east and west.\n\n**Witnesses**\n- **The seven the plan specified:** `forbid`, `permit`, `idle`, `blind`, `branch_1`, `occasion_s1` and `occasion_s1_outside`.\n- **`branch_2`** (violated): the vehicle drives straight from the governed lane into the intersection and makes the whole U-turn there. This tests the \"entered from a governed lane\" route on its own.\n- **`branch_3`** (not applicable): as `forbid`, but the sign is a no-left-turn sign.\n\n**Two things to know**\n- **The plan's `permit` text doesn't work as written.** It describes driving straight through, but then the vehicle never turns, so the rule doesn't apply at all and the verdict is \"not applicable\", not \"compliant\". I built `permit` as a 90° left turn under the sign instead. The planners may want to reword that text.\n- **\"Entered the intersection from lane l\"** is written as: the vehicle was in l at some moment and has been in the intersection at every moment since. A moment where it straddles both counts as being in l.\n\nBefore the first run I also sent one stray `echo` command by mistake. It did nothing and wasn't a gate run; it's noted in `task_result.md`, which is in the working directory.",
